INT. NIGHT-SHIFT NURSES' STATION, ONCOLOGY WARD - NIGHT

A pool of fluorescent light in a dim corridor. Charts, a stale vending-machine coffee, a monitor beeping softly.

NURSE BEATRIX OYELOWO-LANDRY (50s, cardigan over scrubs, glasses on a beaded chain) charts at the desk. Beside her, DR. AUGUSTINE HALLORAN-DIOP (30s, rumpled, third double shift) slumps on a stool. A MAN IN A COAT (60s, visitor's badge, shopping bag) stands at the counter's edge.

BEATRIX
Doctor, there's a gentleman for you.

HALLORAN-DIOP
Who?

BEATRIX
He didn't say.

She looks up at the man. He lowers his eyes.

MAN IN A COAT
I'm sorry. I know it's late.

HALLORAN-DIOP
It's fine. Can I help you?

MAN IN A COAT
I'm Mr. Okpara's brother.

HALLORAN-DIOP
Room 412.

MAN IN A COAT
Yes.

HALLORAN-DIOP
I'm sorry. Mr. Okpara passed away at two.

The man nods. He doesn't move.

OKPARA'S BROTHER
(quietly)
I brought him a sweater.

BEATRIX
That's thoughtful.

OKPARA'S BROTHER
It was his favorite. Blue, with the elbows gone. I thought it might be cold.

HALLORAN-DIOP
Sir, would you like to see him?

OKPARA'S BROTHER
I think I should.

BEATRIX
I'll walk you down.

OKPARA'S BROTHER
Thank you.

HALLORAN-DIOP
Take as long as you need.

OKPARA'S BROTHER
Doctor.

HALLORAN-DIOP
Yes?

OKPARA'S BROTHER
Did he say anything? At the end?

Halloran-Diop looks at Beatrix. She gives the smallest shake of her head.

HALLORAN-DIOP
He asked about the weather.

OKPARA'S BROTHER
(a faint smile)
Of course he did.

He lifts the shopping bag, adjusts its handle, and follows Beatrix down the corridor. The monitor beeps.

HALLORAN-DIOP
(to himself)
It was raining.
